# Task 6 Fix Round 3 Re-review

**Base:** `696980b`  
**Head:** `1f3040d`  
**Verdict:** **NEEDS FIXES — Critical 1, Important 2, Minor 2**

## Scope and method

This was a read-only review of the complete Fix Round 3 section of
`task-6-report.md`, all 3,164 lines of `review-696980b..1f3040d.diff`, every
open item from `task-6-rereview-2.md`, the Task 6 brief, and the relevant
current authorization, gateway, budget, audit, recovery, and focused-test
paths. No tracked file was edited. No provider, network, live pricing, real
SDK, replay/parity, Docker, background process, user authorization, permanent
ledger, or broad test suite was used. No executable reproducer was needed;
the findings below are established by quoted control flow and include exact
fake/temp RED shapes.

Round 3 genuinely closes the caller-owned call-plan drift and the accepted
post-publication retroactive-close race. It also makes genuine predecessor
artifacts line up correctly in the ordinary sequential path. It is not ready:
Task 6 still exposes a public way to manufacture the “authenticated” terminal
receipt on which predecessor authority now rests, audit replay does not verify
the lifecycle semantics behind that receipt, and the restart finalizer cannot
operate with a BudgetLedger from a new process.

## Prior-finding adjudication

| Prior open item | Verdict | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| Critical 1 — predecessor-authenticated, plan-exclusive, one-shot role issuance | **PARTIAL; OPEN.** Genuine accepted predecessor calls are checked against exact facts and artifact digests (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:808-949`), and the ordinary single-threaded bind/capture path is plan-keyed and one-shot (`950-986`). But the durable predecessor receipt is still self-issuable through public Task 6 APIs: `AuditTrail.write_provider_call_receipt` accepts a caller-built `ProviderCallRecord` and caller-chosen accepted payload digest (`agent_loop.py:11477-11512`, `11775-11796`), while `AuthorizationLedger.reconcile_call` trusts the resulting object without consulting AuditTrail (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:2023-2076`). A module-global object token is not an authority boundary (`50`, `435-485`). See Critical 1. |
| Critical 2 — canonical exact call-plan snapshot | **ADDRESSED.** AuthorizationLedger reconstructs and owns the manifest/call copies (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:605-667`). The wrapper authenticates the caller plan once and uses `plan_snapshot` for capture, preflight, both reservations, audit facts, SDK `max_tokens`, response caps, and the returned role call (`agent_loop.py:3371-3446`, `3478-3507`, `3572-3809`). The fourth-clock fake test targets the prior failure accurately (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:4457-4525`). |
| Important 1 — terminal receipt, semantic replay, and restart recovery | **PARTIAL; OPEN.** The receipt now carries both provider-record and terminal-event digests, and generic audit replay rejects malformed hashes and partial framing (`agent_loop.py:11721-11796`, `12013-12070`). However durable authorization replay re-seals the receipt dictionary without checking either artifact against AuditTrail (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:1150-1245`), optimizer recovery accepts a present lifecycle digest without proving that it names the correct reserved/started event (`agent_loop.py:11858-11913`), and a true new-process BudgetLedger cannot be restored. See Important 1 and 2. |
| Important 2 — accepted post-publication interruption/reentrant plan two | **ADDRESSED.** Once budget, terminal audit, and authorization reconciliation all verify, `finalize` marks any accepted publication final (`agent_loop.py:3509-3570`). The outer `BaseException` path therefore does not attempt a cancellation/failed close (`3810-3896`). If the original reconciler raises after publishing, its idempotent record comparison and the verifier preserve exactly one accepted reconciliation (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:2105-2159`). The focused test demonstrates a synchronously reserved plan two and no retroactive lease close (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:5205-5276`). Persistent verifier failure can still propagate, but the outer path does not rewrite the already accepted call. |
| Minor 1 — report metadata/evidence | **PARTIAL; OPEN.** The base/head and commit metadata are now accurate (`task-6-report.md:237-243`). The claims that all findings are closed, that receipt replay is semantic, and that restart recovers BudgetLedger state are not supported (`247-283`, `299-314`, `349-360`). |

## Actionable findings

### Critical 1 — public Task 6 APIs can manufacture an accepted predecessor receipt and authorize the next paid role

**Confidence: 10/10.**

The new authority chain relies on `TerminalAuditReceipt`, whose only origin
check is identity against a module-global object:

```python
if self._audit_seal is not _TERMINAL_AUDIT_RECEIPT_SEAL:
    raise ValueError("terminal audit receipt is not AuditTrail authenticated")
```

at `core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:435-485`; the token itself is importable
at line 50. More importantly, no private-name access is needed. The public
`AuditTrail.write_provider_call_receipt` path accepts any shape-valid
`ProviderCallRecord`, any matching manifest digest, and any caller-chosen
accepted payload SHA-256, writes the provider/terminal artifacts, and returns a
sealed receipt (`agent_loop.py:11477-11512`, `11525-11593`, `11775-11796`). It
does not require a gateway-owned reservation capability, a BudgetLedger
reconciliation, a real provider response, or the preceding reserved/started
audit event.

`AuthorizationLedger.reconcile_call` then checks only receipt type, manifest,
call/iteration/role/outcome, and digest shape before appending the accepted
reconciliation (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:2023-2076`, `2160-2255`).
It never asks AuditTrail to prove receipt origin. Finally, successor issuance
looks only for that reconciliation, exact facts, and the SHA-256 of the supplied
public payload (`855-883`). `PitOptimizerRoleCall` is also publicly constructible
from those three public values (`399-431`).

**Impact:** An authorized caller can skip the investigator provider call,
invent a valid investigator artifact, charge/finalize only AuthorizationLedger
with invented accepted facts, and then bind and transmit an author request as
if the invented artifact came from R1. The same construction composes through
critic and later-investigator lineage. This is a Task 6 authorization bypass,
not merely a Task 7 ordering mistake.

**Required fix:** Make receipt issuance consume an unforgeable, plan-exclusive
gateway/audit lifecycle capability tied to the exact authorization and budget
reservations. Reconciliation must verify the durable receipt against the
specific AuditTrail provider record and terminal event, not trust a Python
class/token or a public writer. Successor issuance should consume an
authenticated predecessor identity produced only by that completed transition.

**Exact fake/temp RED:** Create the normal temp ledger/lease/audit with an empty
fake client. Reserve call 1 only in AuthorizationLedger. Construct a valid
investigator artifact and matching accepted facts, project a caller-built
`ProviderCallRecord`, and call `audit.write_provider_call_receipt(...,
payload_sha256=sha256(artifact), run_manifest_sha256=manifest.sha256,
lifecycle_audit_sha256="a" * 64)` without any SDK call or BudgetLedger call-1
reservation/reconciliation. Pass that receipt to `authorization.reconcile_call`,
construct `PitOptimizerRoleCall(plan_1, artifact, facts)`, and bind a valid
author input with it. Require `AuthorizationError` before author BudgetLedger
reservation/audit/SDK effects and require no accepted call-1 reconciliation.
Current code accepts the invented predecessor and can send the author fake call.

**Why Task 6, not Task 7:** Task 6 defines the receipt constructor/writer, the
authorization reconciler, the predecessor consumer, and the paid gateway. Task
7 cannot turn those public self-authenticating primitives into an unforgeable
capability by calling them in a preferred order.

### Important 1 — hash-valid audit/receipt replay is not a semantic provider-call replay

**Confidence: 10/10.**

Authorization replay reconstructs a `TerminalAuditReceipt` from the ledger and
injects the same module token itself:

```python
replayed_receipt = TerminalAuditReceipt(
    **terminal_audit_receipt,
    _audit_seal=_TERMINAL_AUDIT_RECEIPT_SEAL,
)
```

at `core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:1180-1201`. It verifies the receipt's
terminal-event digest equals the legacy digest field and that manifest/call
facts match (`1226-1245`), but it does not verify `provider_record_sha256`,
`terminal_event_sha256`, or `audit_run_id` against an AuditTrail. In particular,
`audit_run_id` is never required to equal the authenticated manifest run ID.
A hash-valid relabel of only the provider digest or audit run therefore reopens.

Audit replay has a second independent semantic gap. `verify_audit_chain`
validates generic JSON framing, sequence, and hashes (`agent_loop.py:12013-12070`),
but `recover_pit_optimizer_terminal` merely validates a present
`lifecycle_audit_sha256` as 64 hex characters and adopts it (`11890-11904`). It
requires a matching `provider_call_started` event only for the legacy case in
which the lifecycle field is absent (`11872-11897`). It does not prove that a
started call's lifecycle digest names its exact start event, require the
reserved -> started -> terminal order, require the terminal event to be final
for that call, or reject a post-terminal second start marker.

**Impact:** A receipt can remain structurally valid while naming the wrong
provider artifact/audit run, and a replayed audit can claim an accepted or
possibly-sent call without the lifecycle event that proves it. A hash-valid
post-terminal retry marker is ignored. Recovery can then reconcile and unlock
the next plan from a history that Task 6 has not semantically authenticated.

**Required fix:** Reopen authorization and audit as one verified recovery
boundary. Check audit run ID against the manifest; check both receipt digests
against the exact immutable provider file and terminal event; and replay an
optimizer call state machine with one exact reservation, optional/exact start
according to `request_started`, one terminal event after it, and no later event
for the same call. Do not re-seal a serialized receipt until those checks pass.

**Exact fake/temp REDs:**

1. Complete one genuine accepted fake call, change only
   `terminal_audit_receipt.provider_record_sha256` and `audit_run_id` in the
   authorization reconciliation, recompute the authorization record hash chain,
   and reopen AuthorizationLedger while leaving the provider file/audit untouched.
   Require reopen or successor bind to reject against AuditTrail. Current replay
   accepts because neither field participates in its semantic checks.
2. Complete one genuine fake call, append a second
   `provider_call_started` event for the same manifest/call after its terminal
   event, then call `AuditTrail.open_existing` and
   `recover_pit_optimizer_terminal`. Require `AuditError` for a post-terminal
   lifecycle/retry. Current recovery selects the earlier terminal and ignores
   the later start. A sibling RED can write a started record with a random
   lifecycle digest and no matching start event; present-field recovery also
   accepts it.

**Why Task 6, not Task 7:** Task 6 owns the serialized receipt schema,
AuditTrail replay, AuthorizationLedger replay, and the rule that permits the
next paid call. Task 7 cannot validate digests or lifecycle edges that Task 6
does not expose or enforce.

### Important 2 — the “restart” finalizer requires the pre-crash in-memory BudgetLedger and loses the original terminal code

**Confidence: 10/10.**

`BudgetLedger` has no durable open/restore operation; its aggregate fields and
optimizer reservation/reconciliation maps are initialized only in memory
(`agent_loop.py:1733-1768`). Recovery reads the terminal record's
`BudgetSnapshot`, but does not restore anything:

```python
if _budget_snapshot(self.ledger) != expected_budget_snapshot:
    raise AuditError("optimizer recovery budget snapshot differs")
```

at `agent_loop.py:3292-3334`. A BudgetLedger created after process loss is
necessarily the zero snapshot and is rejected. The focused test proves this
failure, then calls the alleged restart path with the original pre-crash
`budget` object (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:5566-5695`). That is an
in-process gateway rewrap, not restart recovery.

The recovered terminal transition is also not semantically exact. The receipt
does not contain the requested lease `terminal_code`
(`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:435-498`), and recovery calls
`reconcile_call` without one (`agent_loop.py:3330-3334`). Authorization therefore
defaults every nonaccepted outcome except provider `budget_exceeded` to
`failed` (`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:1840-1860`). A crash after a
terminal cancellation or a before-send wall-budget exhaustion is replayed as
`failed`, not `cancelled` or `budget_exhausted`.

**Impact:** After a real process crash between terminal audit publication and
authorization reconciliation, the durable active reservation continues to
block the next plan forever unless a caller unsafely reconstructs Task 6's
private accounting state. If it somehow supplies the old aggregate state,
recovery can still publish the wrong terminal history.

**Required fix:** Add an authenticated BudgetLedger reopen/restore operation
that rebuilds the exact aggregate and optimizer reservation/reconciliation
state from durable Task 6 evidence, validates ledger maxima/run identity, and is
idempotent. Bind the intended terminal code into the terminal event/receipt and
pass the exact recovered value into reconciliation. Keep the next call fenced
until both ledgers reach that exact postcondition.

**Exact fake/temp REDs:**

1. Publish Budget reconciliation and the terminal provider event, stop before
   authorization reconciliation, discard every gateway/BudgetLedger object,
   construct a fresh BudgetLedger with the same configured maxima, reopen only
   AuditTrail and AuthorizationLedger from disk, and recover. Require exact
   Budget snapshot restoration, one authorization reconciliation, and either
   the next-plan reservation or the required terminal close. Current code raises
   `AuditError("optimizer recovery budget snapshot differs")`, and the next
   reservation remains blocked by the active authorization fence.
2. Repeat the existing before-send recovery setup with `KeyboardInterrupt` as
   the original failure (or wall-budget exhaustion), crash after the terminal
   event, and recover. Require `lease_close.terminal_code == "cancelled"` (or
   `"budget_exhausted"`). Current recovery emits `"failed"` because the intended
   code was never persisted.

**Why Task 6, not Task 7:** BudgetLedger internals, terminal provider evidence,
and authorization close semantics are Task 6 interfaces. Task 7 must not invent
or mutate accounting fields, and it cannot recover a terminal code that Task 6
discarded.

### Minor 1 — plan-exclusive bind issuance is not synchronized

**Confidence: 9/10.**

The file lock protecting predecessor reads ends at
`core/pit_optimizer_authorization.py:841-883`. The process-local exclusivity
check and assignment at `950-961`, and the consumed check/pop/add at `971-985`,
use no shared threading lock. Two distinct concurrent binders can both observe
`prior is None`, both return success, and overwrite the same plan slot. The paid
consumer remains fail-closed in CPython because only one `dict.pop` returns the
entry, so this is not a second provider-call authorization; it does falsify the
reported “distinct objects cannot bind” guarantee and leaves schedule-dependent
capability ownership.

**Fix:** Guard bind, capture, and consumed-state verification with one
per-ledger `threading.Lock` and make check+insert / check+pop+mark atomic.

**Focused RED shape:** Use a barrier around two temp/fake bind calls for equal
but distinct investigator objects and require exactly one success. Current
check-then-set permits both bind calls to return under the forced interleaving.

### Minor 2 — Round 3 report and tests overstate the receipt/restart evidence

**Confidence: 10/10.**

- `task-6-report.md:241-243` says all findings are resolved, which conflicts
  with the public receipt issuer and non-restoring restart path above.
- The receipt tamper test changes `role`, a field explicitly checked against the
  reservation (`tests/test_pit_optimizer_v2.py:5496-5563`). It does not change
  provider/event digests or audit-run identity, and it never cross-verifies the
  authorization receipt against reopened AuditTrail.
- The restart test correctly rejects a fresh BudgetLedger, then reuses the
  original object (`5566-5695`). It is evidence of a durable fence, not evidence
  that a new process can recover BudgetLedger.
- The accepted-publication test reserves plan two only in AuthorizationLedger
  (`5205-5276`). It honestly proves no retroactive close, but not the report's
  broader claim that plan-two BudgetLedger/audit state is coherent.
- The canonical-plan, ordinary predecessor-artifact, partial-tail, and accepted
  no-retroactive-close tests are otherwise honest for the paths they exercise.
- The reported `207 passed` and Ruff output were inspected but not independently
  rerun during this read-only review.

## New breakage

- The unsynchronized plan-slot registry is newly introduced by Round 3. It is a
  Minor issuance race because the paid capture still consumes at most one entry.
- No new Critical/Important regression was found in canonical plan use, exact
  pricing, old grant isolation, named-grant ceilings, one-request/max-retries
  behavior, accepted post-publication no-close semantics, legacy
  ProviderCallRecord readability, or content secrecy.
- Critical 1 and Important 1/2 are incomplete closures of the prior
  predecessor-authentication and restart-recovery findings, not Task 7 wiring.

## Requirement coverage

| Task 6 requirement | Status after Round 3 |
|---|---|
| Hash-chain/exclusive-lock durability and atomic grant+window replacement | **Meets; unchanged.** The authorization ledger still uses its exclusive file lock and complete-image replacement. Audit hash replay now rejects partial final framing, but semantic lifecycle replay remains partial (Important 1). |
| Old 20/19 isolation, explicit named grants, most-restrictive ceilings | **Meets; unchanged.** No Round 3 path infers or combines stale capacity. |
| Authenticated manifest/requirement/source scope; non-secret approval | **Partial.** Manifest-owned call plans and source reconstruction meet. Non-source predecessor authority is self-issuable through the receipt boundary (Critical 1). |
| One-shot run key, concurrent/reopen rules, no-spend lease open | **Meets at lease open.** Durable call finalization/restart remains incomplete (Important 2); process-local role bind issuance has a Minor race. |
| Frozen Decimal R1 pricing identity and no post-freeze drift | **Meets.** Round 3 does not weaken the already-correct live pricing reauthentication/copy. |
| Next-call-only reservation; exact call indexes/iterations/roles | **Partial.** Canonical plan consumption is fixed. Fabricated accepted predecessor receipts can advance the next call without the required R1 predecessor (Critical 1). |
| Complete bytes/tokens/USD before any SDK/ledger/start side effect | **Meets in `request_pit_optimizer_once`.** All paid arguments use captured bytes and canonical plan/pricing before reservation. The public receipt bypass is an authorization-origin defect, not a preflight calculation defect. |
| BudgetLedger and AuthorizationLedger consistency for every exception/cancellation | **Partial / blocked.** Ordinary and accepted post-publication paths are coherent. A new-process BudgetLedger cannot recover, and recovered terminal code is not exact (Important 2). |
| All-R1 isolated role profile; exactly one SDK request; `max_retries=0`; no healing/recovery | **Meets in reviewed gateway code.** No Round 3 change adds a retry, alternate model, healing, or generation recovery. |
| Iteration-aware closed ProviderCallRecord/audit; legacy readability | **Partial.** Both receipt digests exist and legacy records remain readable. Receipt/audit semantic replay is incomplete (Important 1). |
| Terminal lease closure; no disguised retry | **Partial / blocked.** Normal nonaccepted and verified accepted paths are stable. Restart can wedge or mislabel closure, and semantic replay ignores a post-terminal start marker (Important 1/2). |
| Content, credential, source, and provider-response secrecy | **Meets in reviewed persistence.** Terminal artifacts remain closed over accounting/lifecycle/digests; prompt/source/raw response/header/credential/approval text is absent. |
| Record-grant CLI cannot infer authorization, combine stale capacity, or touch credentials | **Meets; unchanged.** Round 3 does not touch this boundary. |

## Test and report audit

- All added product tests use injected fake clients/pricing and pytest temporary
  roots. No new test imports or invokes a real SDK/provider/live-pricing path.
- Canonical plan mutation is tested at the correct late callback and asserts the
  SDK cap, returned plan, and authorization reservation.
- Ordinary author/critic/later-investigator lineage tests use genuine accepted
  fake predecessors and catch artifact relabeling. They do not authenticate the
  public receipt issuer or concurrent bind issuance.
- Partial-tail framing is tested honestly. Semantic call-event ordering,
  provider/event digest cross-replay, wrong audit run ID, and post-terminal
  lifecycle events are not covered.
- The restart test is intentionally same-process for BudgetLedger despite its
  name/report framing; a true fresh-ledger RED is missing.
- Accepted post-publication behavior is sufficiently supported by code and the
  focused test for the prior no-retroactive-close finding, but the report should
  narrow its plan-two cross-ledger claim.

## Resource and secrecy audit

- This review used no provider/network/live pricing/SDK/replay/container,
  background helper, permanent ledger, authorization, or external effect.
- No temporary reproducer was created, so no temp root or helper process remains.
- No tracked file changed; this report is under the ignored SDD workspace.
- The reviewed provider record, terminal event, and receipt disclose only closed
  lifecycle/accounting/model/pricing/digest facts. No new content or credential
  persistence leak was found.
- Receipt fabrication is an authority failure, not a secrecy leak: it uses
  caller-selected closed facts/digests and requires no provider credential.

## Out-of-scope observations

- Live OpenRouter/SDK compatibility remains intentionally untested.
- Windows directory-fsync/power-loss strengthening remains a separate platform
  hardening item; it is not the cause of the semantic replay or BudgetLedger
  restore findings.
- Task 7 still owns orchestration and supplies genuine predecessor calls. It
  does not own receipt authenticity, audit replay, BudgetLedger restoration, or
  terminal reconciliation semantics.

## Final disposition

**NEEDS FIXES.** Round 3 fixes exact call-plan consumption, genuine sequential
artifact matching, both digest fields, partial-tail detection, and accepted
post-publication no-retroactive-close behavior. It remains blocked by one
Critical public receipt/predecessor-minting path and two Important durability
gaps: audit receipts are not semantically replayed against their call lifecycle,
and a true process restart cannot restore BudgetLedger or the original terminal
code.
